---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
    title="tcplot | Claire Nyquist"
    description="tcplot — a meetup group for data visualization enthusiasts exploring creative and scientific plotting."
    image="/images/tcplot(whale).webp"
>
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8">
        <!-- Header -->
        <div class="text-center mb-12">
            <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl name-gradient mb-6">
                <span class="font-silkscreen font-normal">tcplot</span>
            </h1>
            <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 max-w-3xl mx-auto leading-relaxed mb-4">
                <span class="font-silkscreen">tcplot</span> is a Twin Cities community for data visualization
                practitioners, enthusiasts, and storytellers. Whether you are a
                D3 wizard, a Tableau pro, a ggplot2 devotee, or someone who
                appreciates a well-crafted chart, you've found your people.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Colors Image -->
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-12">
            <div class="max-w-2xl w-full rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
                <img
                    src="/images/tcplot(colorswithtext).webp"
                    alt="tcplot data visualization twin cities — colorful mosaic collage of rectangular chart elements"
                    class="w-full h-auto object-contain"
                />
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- About Section -->
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-12">
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Our mission is to elevate the data literacy and design standards
                of the MSP area through three core pillars:
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-6">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span
                        ><strong class="text-gray-900">Talks:</strong> Hear from local
                        experts and industry leaders on the "why" and "how" of great
                        visualization.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span
                        ><strong class="text-gray-900">Workshops:</strong> Roll up
                        your sleeves for hands-on sessions where we master new tools,
                        libraries, and design principles.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span
                        ><strong class="text-gray-900">Build-a-thons:</strong> Join
                        collaborative coworking where we take datasets and turn them
                        into impactful, interactive, or artistic visual stories.</span
                    >
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Links -->
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-6 mt-12">
            <a
                href="https://tcplot.com"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
            >
                tcplot.com
            </a>
            <a
                href="https://www.instagram.com/tcplot/"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
            >
                Instagram
            </a>
            <a
                href="https://www.meetup.com/tcplot/?eventOrigin=home_groups_you_organize"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
            >
                Meetup
            </a>
        </div>

        <!-- Event: A Year in Your Own Data Build-a-thon -->
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-16 mb-12">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">
                Upcoming: A Year in Your Own Data — A Build-a-thon
            </h2>

            <div class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
                <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">
                    Saturday, July 25th · 10:00 AM to 12:00 PM CDT
                </p>
                <p class="mt-1">The Coffee Shop NE, 2852 Johnson St NE, Minneapolis, MN</p>
            </div>

            <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 italic">
                You've been collecting data all year. Let's make something out of it.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Join <span class="font-silkscreen">tcplot</span> for a build-a-thon where the dataset is <em>you</em>. Bring a year of your own data — steps, sleep, songs, spending, miles, messages, books, photos — and spend the session turning it into a visualization worth keeping. Code it, draw it, or build it by hand.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                The best personal-data projects aren't about the numbers; they're about the story only you can tell. A year is long enough to hold a season, a habit, a heartbreak, a move. We'll help you find the one thread worth pulling.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                What you'll do
            </h3>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-6">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span>Bring — or pull on the spot — a year of your own personal data</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span>Pick the one question you actually want it to answer</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span>Build a visualization — interactive, static, or physical — solo or in a small group</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span>Share what you made and what it told you</span>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Where to find a year of your data
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Most of it is already sitting in an app, waiting for an export — health apps (Apple Health, Google Fit, Fitbit), your <a href="https://www.spotify.com/us/account/privacy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Spotify streaming history</a>, <a href="https://takeout.google.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Google Takeout</a>, a year of bank transactions, Strava or Garmin, Letterboxd or Goodreads, or your phone's screen-time report. Some exports take a day or two to arrive, so request yours before the event. Your data stays yours — bring only what you're comfortable working with.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                What to bring
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Your laptop and charger, a year of your data (or be ready to download it), and whatever tools you love — D3, Tableau, ggplot2, Observable, a spreadsheet, or just paper and beads. No tools at all is fine too.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Who this is for
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Anyone curious about their own life in data — seasoned coders and total beginners alike. Come build something only you could make.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                Hope to see you there!
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Event: Data Collection in the Park -->
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-16 mb-12">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">
                Upcoming: Data Collection &amp; Visualization in the Park
            </h2>

            <div class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
                <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">
                    Saturday, August 29th · 10:00 AM to 12:00 PM CDT
                </p>
                <p class="mt-1">Meet at The Coffee Shop NE, 2852 Johnson St NE, Minneapolis, MN</p>
            </div>

            <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 italic">
                The best dataset is the one you gather yourself.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Join <span class="font-silkscreen">tcplot</span> for a morning that starts with coffee and ends with a chart. We'll meet at The Coffee Shop NE, walk over to Audubon Park to collect data by hand, then come back and turn what we gathered into a visualization.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Data collection is usually invisible — by the time most of us see a dataset, someone else has already decided what counts. This is a chance to make those decisions yourself: what to measure, how to tally it, and what gets left out. The constraints of a clipboard and forty minutes turn out to be the whole lesson.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                How the morning will flow
            </h3>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-6">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span><strong class="text-gray-900">Coffee &amp; plan.</strong> Meet at The Coffee Shop NE for quick intros and to decide what each of us will count.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span><strong class="text-gray-900">Collect.</strong> Walk to Audubon Park and spend about forty minutes gathering data — tallies, timings, sketches, observations.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span><strong class="text-gray-900">Visualize.</strong> Head back and turn your raw tallies into something readable — on paper, in code, or in beads and thread.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span><strong class="text-gray-900">Share.</strong> Compare what we made — and how differently we each saw the same park.</span>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Things you could count
            </h3>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-6">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span>Birds, squirrels, and dogs — by count, by type, by minute</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span>How people pass through: walking, running, biking, rolling, with strollers</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span>Trees by species, or the colors of the leaves</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span>Dandelions in the grass — where they cluster and where they don't</span>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                What to bring
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Something to record with — a notebook or your phone — and shoes for a short walk. A laptop is welcome for the visualizing part but not required; we'll have paper and making supplies on hand. Dress for the weather.
            </p>
            <p class="text-base text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Rain plan: light rain, we'll go anyway. Heavy rain, we'll collect our data right from The Coffee Shop NE.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Who this is for
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Anyone curious about where data comes from — practitioners and first-timers alike. No tools or experience required, just a willingness to notice things.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                Hope to see you there!
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Event: Data Physicalization Workshop -->
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-16 mb-12">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">
                Past Event: Data Physicalization Workshop — June 27th
            </h2>

            <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 italic">
                What if your next chart wasn't on a screen?
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Join <span class="font-silkscreen">tcplot</span> for a hands-on morning of data physicalization — translating real datasets into objects you can hold, turn over, and put on your desk. We'll explore paper, thread, beads, and the surprisingly hard question of what to encode when you can't reach for a library.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                We'll draw inspiration from projects like Giorgia Lupi and Stefanie Posavec's <em>Dear Data</em>, Nathalie Miebach's weather sculptures, and the broader tradition of data physicalization (<a href="https://dataphys.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">dataphys.org</a>). The constraint is the point: when you can't pipe data into ggplot, you have to make real decisions about hierarchy, encoding, and what's actually worth showing.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                What you'll do
            </h3>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-6">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span>Pick from a handful of small, pre-prepared datasets (<a href="https://www.dnr.state.mn.us/climate/historical/daily.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Twin Cities weather</a>, <a href="https://science.ebird.org/en/use-ebird-data/download-ebird-data-products" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">MN bird sightings</a>, <a href="https://opendata.minneapolismn.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">library checkouts</a>, a week of your own personal data, or any other data)</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span>Work solo or in pairs to translate it into a physical artifact</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span>Share what you made and what surprised you</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span>Leave with the object you built</span>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                What we'll provide
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Cardstock, embroidery thread, beads, tape, markers, making supplies, datasets printed and ready to go.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                What to bring
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Yourself. Optionally, a week of personal data you've been tracking (sleep, coffee, steps, moods, anything) or your own craft supplies of choice.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Who this is for
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Anyone curious about data visualization — practitioners working in D3, Tableau, ggplot2, Observable, or none of the above. No craft skills required.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                Hope to see you there!
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Event: Claude Code Workshop -->
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-16 mb-12">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">
                Past Event: Claude Code for Data Visualization — May 23rd
            </h2>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                AI-assisted coding is changing how visualization practitioners
                prototype, iterate, and ship. In this hands-on workshop, we'll
                get you up and running with Claude Code — Anthropic's agentic
                coding tool — and put it to work on real dataviz problems. No
                prior experience with AI coding tools required.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Whether you're deep in D3, grinding through ggplot2, or
                somewhere in between, you'll leave with a working setup and a
                concrete sense of where Claude Code fits (and doesn't fit) in
                a practitioner's workflow.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Location & Logistics
            </h3>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span
                        ><strong class="text-gray-900">When:</strong> Saturday,
                        May 23 · 10:00 AM to 12:00 PM CDT</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span
                        ><strong class="text-gray-900">Where:</strong> The Coffee
                        Shop NE, 2852 Johnson St NE, Minneapolis, MN</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span>Make sure to bring your laptop and charger.</span>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Event: API Build-a-thon -->
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-16 mb-12">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">
                Past Event: An API Build-a-thon on April 4th
            </h2>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Are you ready to build a digital masterpiece? Whether you want
                to curate a virtual gallery using The Met, track near-earth
                objects with NASA, or dive into the digital archives of the
                Natural History Museum, this is your playground.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                There's no set prompt—only a treasure trove of world-class APIs
                and a room full of creative minds. You pick the data, you pick
                the stack, and create your own adventure.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                The Mission
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                You have one day to build a project using at least one culture,
                science, or other cool API. We'll provide a curated list of fun
                APIs to get your gears turning, but the final destination is up
                to you.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Potential Quests
            </h3>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-8">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span
                        ><strong class="text-gray-900">The Curator:</strong> Build
                        a "Daily Masterpiece" bot using <a
                            href="https://metmuseum.github.io/"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >The Metropolitan Museum of Art API</a
                        >.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span
                        ><strong class="text-gray-900">The Astronaut:</strong> Create
                        a live asteroid tracker or a Mars Rover gallery using <a
                            href="https://api.nasa.gov/"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >NASA Open APIs</a
                        >.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span
                        ><strong class="text-gray-900">The Explorer:</strong> Map
                        out biodiversity hotspots using the <a
                            href="https://www.gbif.org/developer/summary"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >Global Biodiversity Information Facility (GBIF)</a
                        >.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">▸</span>
                    <span
                        ><strong class="text-gray-900">The Historian:</strong> Connect
                        the dots of the past using the <a
                            href="https://www.si.edu/openaccess"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Smithsonian</a
                        > or <a
                            href="https://www.loc.gov/apis/"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >Library of Congress</a
                        > data.</span
                    >
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                More Cool APIs
            </h3>
            <div
                class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-x-8 gap-y-2 text-gray-600 mb-8"
            >
                <div>
                    <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-3 mb-2">
                        Science & Nature
                    </h4>
                    <ul class="space-y-1">
                        <li>
                            ▸ <a
                                href="https://api.nasa.gov/"
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                                class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                                >NASA APIs</a
                            > — APOD, Mars rovers, asteroids
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            ▸ <a
                                href="https://www.gbif.org/developer/summary"
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                                class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">GBIF</a
                            > — Global biodiversity data
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            ▸ <a
                                href="https://openweathermap.org/api"
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                                class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                                >OpenWeatherMap</a
                            > — Weather data
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            ▸ <a
                                href="https://earthquake.usgs.gov/fdsnws/event/1/"
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                                class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                                >USGS Earthquake API</a
                            > — Real-time quake data
                        </li>
                    </ul>

                    <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-3 mb-2">
                        Health & Public Data
                    </h4>
                    <ul class="space-y-1">
                        <li>
                            ▸ <a
                                href="https://www.who.int/data/gho/info/gho-odata-api"
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                                class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                                >WHO GHO API</a
                            > — Global health data
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            ▸ <a
                                href="https://wonder.cdc.gov/"
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                                class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                                >CDC WONDER</a
                            > — Public health datasets
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            ▸ <a
                                href="https://open.fda.gov/apis/"
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                                class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">OpenFDA</a
                            > — Drug events, recalls, labeling
                        </li>
                    </ul>

                    <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-3 mb-2">
                        Space & Astronomy
                    </h4>
                    <ul class="space-y-1">
                        <li>
                            ▸ <a
                                href="https://github.com/r-spacex/SpaceX-API"
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                                class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                                >SpaceX API</a
                            > — Launches, rockets, capsules
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            ▸ <a
                                href="http://open-notify.org/Open-Notify-API/"
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                                class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                                >Open Notify</a
                            > — ISS location, astronauts in space
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-3 mb-2">
                        Culture & Fun
                    </h4>
                    <ul class="space-y-1">
                        <li>
                            ▸ <a
                                href="https://data.rijksmuseum.nl/docs/"
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                                class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                                >Rijksmuseum API</a
                            > — Art collection data
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            ▸ <a
                                href="https://openlibrary.org/developers/api"
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                                class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                                >Open Library API</a
                            > — Book metadata
